NUMBER TIME ip.src -> ip.dst TRANSFER_PROTOCOL_BEING_USED PACKET_SIZE_IN_BYTES \
[tshark warning] SOURCE_PORT DESTINATION_PORT TCP_FLAG SEQUENCE_NUMBER \
ACKNOWLEDGEMENT_NUMBER WINDOW_SIZE PAYLOAD_LENGTH TIMESTAMP_VALUE TIMESTAMP_ECHO_REPLY

NUBMER                       | Packet number in the capture.
TIME                         | Timestamp in seconds since the capture started.
ip.src                       | Source IP. Who sent the packet.
->                           | Direction of the packet.
ip.dst                       | Who the packet is going to.
TRANSFER_PROTOCOL_BEING_USED | The tranport protocol being used.
PACKET_SIZE_IN_BYTES         | Packet size in bytes(including headers).
[tshark warning]             | Tshark warning.
SOURCE_PORT                  | Who is sending from port.
DESTINATION_PORT             | Who the getting on port.
TCP_FLAG                     | TCP flag(
                             |  [SYN] - Start connection
                             |  [SYN, ACK] - Accept connection
                             |  [ACK] - Acknowledge received data
                             |  [FIN] - Close connection
                             |  [RST] - Reset/Abort connection
                             |  [PSH] - Push data immediately
                             |).
SEQUENCE_NUMBER              | Sequence number - TCP uses this to orde packets.
ACKNOWLEDGEMENT_NUMBER       | Confirms what data has been successfully received.
WINDOW_SIZE                  | TCP flow control. Actual size is multiplied by a window
                             | scale factor negotiated at handshake - real window is
                             | likely much larger.
PAYLOAD_LENGTH               | This a pure ACK packet, just an acknowledgement with
                             | no data attached.
TIMESTAMP_VALUE              | Used for round-trip time measurement and to protect
                             | against old duplicate packets.
TIMESTAMP_ECHO_REPLY         | Website is echoing back the timestamp from last packet.
                             | The difference between TSval and TSecr lets TCP
                             | calculate RTT precisely.

